# pstack in T3 Code

This installation updates Cursor's pstack skills for T3 Code and Codex. Apply these tool and configuration mappings when a bundled skill or guide describes Cursor behavior. The host's current instructions and tool schemas take precedence.

## Commands and files

`p3-mode`, `p3-help`, and `setup-p3` retain their installed names. `poteto-mode`, `poteto-help`, and `setup-pstack` are aliases for them. Skills live in `~/.agents/skills`. Supporting guides and agent briefs live in `~/.agents/pstack`.

Invoke skills by name or read their `SKILL.md`. Cursor's plugin installation commands, mode toggles, and `alwaysApply` rule metadata do not configure T3 Code. Use the installed skill catalog. For skill authoring, use the available `skill-creator` skill in place of Cursor's `create-skill`.

## Models

Use the role overrides in the active AGENTS.md instructions. Read `~/.codex/pstack-models.md` for the saved configuration. An explicitly selected project configuration can override the saved global file. Preserve the configured models, repeated panel entries, `default effort: session`, and `session hook` setting.

The upstream role `hardest tasks` corresponds to this installation's `strongest judgment` role. Treat them as aliases. The existing `gpt-6.1-sol` choices override upstream Claude and Grok defaults. A configured panel entry always counts as one seat, including repeated models.

Model identifiers are opaque. Do not append Cursor effort or speed suffixes. Use the host's separate reasoning options and inherit the parent effort when configured as `session`. Do not silently substitute another model or provider when a requested choice is unavailable. Report the limitation and follow the current host instructions. Never rewrite model choices while merely loading or updating skills.

## Delegation and history

Translate Cursor `Task`, `subagent_type`, `run_in_background`, and agent-message instructions to the host's supported orchestration tools. For native subagents, use the native spawn and wait tools when the host permits them. Use `delegate_task` for background agents, other providers, requested settings, or isolated checkouts. Call `orchestrator_capabilities` before the first delegation. Follow a running delegate's returned `next` instruction. Do not replace that wait with polling.

